item msg_011CgZjgf1CH4nLTcUkP7ydn:0 message final at=1793423317972 text="Rope-making is one of the oldest crafts, with roots reaching back tens of thousands of years. Archaeologists have found twisted plant fibers at sites such as Lascaux, where cave paintings date to roughly 17,000 years ago, and impressions of cord on pottery and clay suggest that people were making string and rope well before the advent of agriculture. Early ropes were made from plant materials like flax, hemp, grasses, bark, and sinew, twisted by hand into strands and then combined into larger cords. This simple technique of spinning fibers together was foundational to hunting nets, fishing lines, shelter construction, and the binding of tools.\n\nAs civilizations developed, rope became central to trade, shipping, and engineering. Ancient Egyptians used rope extensively for hauling stone blocks and rigging boats, and tomb paintings show rope-makers at work with twisting frames. Rope production grew into a specialized trade, with long \"rope walks\" built in Greece, Rome, and later Europe, where workers walked backward while feeding fibers into a line of spinning hooks. Hemp and flax were the most valued materials for their strength and durability, and the demands of seafaring made rope a strategic resource, since ships required vast quantities of cordage for sails, rigging, and anchor lines.\n\nThe Industrial Revolution transformed rope-making. Machines replaced hand-spinning in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries, and steel wire rope, first developed for mining and later used in bridges and elevators, became a major innovation. In the twentieth century, synthetic fibers such as nylon, polyester, and polypropylene reshaped the industry, offering greater strength, resistance to rot, and lower weight than natural fibers. Traditional rope-making persists today in heritage workshops and among craftspeople who value the older methods, even as modern ropes serve in climbing, rescue, aviation, and countless other applications."
