item msg_011CgZjh1v35yw1NMpS86xZM:0 message final at=1793423322745 text="Knots are as old as rope itself, and humans have tied them for practical, ceremonial, and artistic purposes for thousands of years. Ancient Egyptian, Chinese, and Incan cultures all left evidence of knotting, from ceremonial cords to record-keeping systems. The Incas used knotted strings called quipu to record numerical and possibly narrative information, a reminder that knots could also serve as a form of writing. Sailors, climbers, and craftspeople later developed specialized knots, each suited to a particular load, material, or task.\n\nOver time, knot-tying became an essential skill in seafaring, where mastering hitches, bends, and splices could mean the difference between a secure ship and a lost one. In the nineteenth and twentieth centuries, mathematicians began studying knots as abstract objects, giving rise to knot theory, a branch of topology that classifies knots by whether one can be deformed into another without cutting the string. Today, knots remain vital in climbing, rescue, sailing, and medicine, and their study continues to connect practical craft with modern science."
